  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nPersistent upper high remains fixed over the Southern Rockies, \nand will sit there through at least the remainder of late July. \nThis will drive the weather pattern through the week ahead. GFS \n500-mb height anomalies suggest this high is 3-4 standard \ndeviations stronger than is typical for the middle of the North \nAmerican Monsoon. The pattern is not favorable for widespread \nrain, as any storms that do form will be mostly driven by recycled\nmoisture and convective temperature/terrain forcing producing \nmeager instability. The monsoonal plume will be broad and diffuse,\nbut generally favor the AZ/NM state line. Precipitable water \nvalues are just over an inch through much of southwest New Mexico,\nwith drier air further to the east across far west Texas and \nsoutheast New Mexico. \n\nWe may see an uptick in storm chances on Tuesday, driven by a\ndecaying MCS over Sonora flushing in additional moisture out of\nthe southwest. NAM/GFS both show an outflow boundary with higher \nsurface dewpoints across southwest New Mexico by sunrise tomorrow \nand afternoon precipitable water values 1.2-1.4\". This will lead \nto more scattered (50-70% coverage) thunderstorms Tuesday evening.\nA few storms may be severe, with large hail and damaging winds.\nCloud-layer flow of 15-knots suggests flooding risk may be lower\nthan the past few days, as storms will be able to more efficiently\nmove to the WNW. Storms may last well into the nighttime hours \ndue to outflow forcing.\n\nThe main headline for the desert lowlands, however, will be hot \ntemperatures. Suffocating upper ridge aloft will promote very hot \nafternoon conditions, followed by little relief overnight. Highs \nwill run 8-12 degrees above normal for late July, which means \nlowland highs in the 102 to 106 degree range through at least next\nFriday. A Heat Advisory is in effect for El Paso and the lower \nRio Grande Valley through Tuesday, and may need to be expanded in \nboth time and area later this week.\n\nMinimal changes in the strength or orientation of the high for \nTuesday onward will make it difficult to pinpoint any appreciable \nday-to-day changes with sensible weather elements. Overall \nweather pattern will be warmer-than-normal temperatures and \nisolated rain chances focused mainly across southwest New Mexico\nand the southern NM high elevations. GFS/ECMWF both agree in a \nslight retrograde of the upper high as we head into the first week\nof August, which may allow for slightly better storm coverage and\nsome interesting north flow aloft.",
